1
00:00:00,360 --> 00:00:03,690
 Recall our shortcut for building the Laplacian.

2
00:00:03,690 --> 00:00:07,670
 Start by throwing out the directions on all the directed edges.

3
00:00:07,670 --> 00:00:11,790
 The diagonals of the Laplacian are just the degrees of each vertex.

4
00:00:11,790 --> 00:00:14,788
 So for instance vertex zero has one, two, three,

5
00:00:14,788 --> 00:00:17,550
 four, five, six, seven incident edges.

6
00:00:17,550 --> 00:00:20,990
 Therefore there's a seven in the zero, zero position.

7
00:00:20,990 --> 00:00:24,030
 Now the off diagonals mark at the edges.

8
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:29,340
 For instance vertex zero is connected to every vertex except seven.

9
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:33,832
 So it has a minus one in all the positions of row zero, except for

10
00:00:33,832 --> 00:00:35,340
 column seven.

11
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:39,160
 As a sanity check on the answer, you can do some simple tests.

12
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:43,820
 For instance, the matrix ought to be symmetric, look symmetric.

13
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:46,460
 Symmetry follows from the definition of the Laplacian

14
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:49,830
 in terms of the incidence matrix.

15
00:00:49,830 --> 00:00:51,970
 Symmetry.

16
00:00:51,970 --> 00:00:57,240
 Here's another sanity check, the rows and the columns should each sum to zero.

17
00:00:57,240 --> 00:00:57,240
 So does that hold for this solution?

